Absolutely, friend. Let's go deeper. As an offensive security researcher, you should understand Boolean operators, logical operators, comparison operators, bitwise operators and compound assignment operators because they control how software evaluates conditions and makes decisions.

One important distinction: not all operators below are Boolean operators in the strict mathematical sense. Some operate on Boolean values, while others produce Boolean results or manipulate bits.

### 1\. Fundamental Boolean algebra operators

These are the seven fundamental Boolean functions.

| Operator | Symbol | Meaning                   |
| -------- | ------ | ------------------------- |
| AND      |        | Both must be TRUE         |
| OR       |        | At least one must be TRUE |
| NOT      |        | Reverses TRUE/FALSE       |
| XOR      |        | Inputs must differ        |
| NAND     |        | NOT AND                   |
| NOR      |        | NOT OR                    |
| XNOR     |        | Inputs must be equal      |

### 2\. Advanced logical operators

| Operator        | Symbol | Meaning             |
| --------------- | ------ | ------------------- |
| Implication     |        | If A, then B        |
| Biconditional   |        | A if and only if B  |
| Converse        |        | Reverse implication |
| Inverse         |        | Negate both sides   |
| Contrapositive  |        | Reverse and negate  |
| Non-implication |        | A does not imply B  |
| Exclusive NOR   | XNOR   | Logical equality    |
| Tautology       |        | Always TRUE         |
| Contradiction   |        | Always FALSE        |

### 3\. Comparison operators

These compare two values and generally return a Boolean result.

| Operator | Meaning                         | Example   |
| -------- | ------------------------------- | --------- |
| ==       | Equal                           | 5 == 5    |
| !=       | Not equal                       | 5 != 3    |
| >        | Greater than                    | 8 > 4     |
| <        | Less than                       | 2 < 7     |
| >=       | Greater than or equal           | 5 >= 5    |
| <=       | Less than or equal              | 3 <= 5    |
| ===      | Strict equality in JavaScript   | 5 === 5   |
| !==      | Strict inequality in JavaScript | 5 !== "5" |

### 4\. Bitwise operators

These operate on individual binary bits. They are particularly important in low-level programming, reverse engineering and binary analysis.

| Operator             | Symbol | Meaning                                  |
| -------------------- | ------ | ---------------------------------------- |
| Bitwise AND          | &      | AND each bit                             |
| Bitwise OR           | \\\|   | OR each bit                              |
| Bitwise XOR          | ^      | XOR each bit                             |
| Bitwise NOT          | ~      | Invert each bit                          |
| Left shift           | <<     | Shift bits left                          |
| Right shift          | >>     | Shift bits right                         |
| Unsigned right shift | >>>    | Shift right with zero-fill in JavaScript |

### 5\. Compound logical operators

These combine multiple conditions.

| Operator | Example | Meaning | |---|---|---| | AND | `A && B` | Both conditions | | OR | `A || B` | At least one condition | | NOT | `!A` | Reverse condition | | XOR | `A ^ B` | Different Boolean values | | NAND | `!(A && B)` | Negated AND | | NOR | `!(A || B)` | Negated OR | | XNOR | `A === B` | Equal Boolean values |

### 6\. Other logical constructs

These are useful when studying complex application logic.

* Short-circuit evaluation

* Conditional expressions (ternary operator)

* Nullish coalescing (`??`)

* Null coalescing assignment (`??=`)

* Logical AND assignment (`&&=`)

* Logical OR assignment (`||=`)

* Optional chaining (`?.`)

These are language features rather than additional fundamental Boolean algebra operators.

